    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-4106",
      "problem": "The point $P$ with coordinates $(3,4)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x)$. Find a point that must lie on $y=f(x)+3$?",
      "worked_solution": "**Method 1**\n\nThe graph of $y = f(x)+3$ is obtained by translating the graph of $y=f(x)$ by $3$ units *up*. \n\nHence, if the point $(3,4)$ lies on $y=f(x)$, then the point \n\n$$\n(3,4+3) = (3,7)\n$$\n\nlies on $y=f(x) + 3.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14218283/assets/577911187229d6a56a8885e4cea2e25752615ed7bb807768abf4b54c5073d177.png)\n\n**Method 2**\n\nAs $(3,4)$ lies on $y = f(x)$, we have that $f(3) = 4.$\n\nNow, if $x = 3$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =f(3)+3 \\\\  & =4+3 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the point $(3,7)$ must lie on $y = f(x) + 3.$",
